+### Lens model depth of field
+
+With **Lens Model DOF** on (the default), the renderer stops being handed a focus *range* and
+is handed a lens. The signed circle of confusion is the thin lens, straight:
+
+```
+c_mm(d) = f^2 / (N * (d_f - f)) * (d - d_f) / d          signed DIAMETER on the sensor
+c_px    = c_mm * frameWidth_px / sensorWidth_mm
+r_unit  = 0.5 * c_px / U      U = 4 * max(W/1920, H/1080) native px of disc RADIUS per unit
+```
+
+Positive is behind focus (far layer), negative in front (near layer) - the near/far split falls
+out of the sign instead of being two separately authored ramps. `N` is the *geometric* aperture,
+so a shape mask in front of the lens shrinks the disc exactly as an iris does. Field curvature
+bends `d` before the formula and edge softness floors the far side after it, both unchanged in
+meaning. `Focus Falloff`, the derived focus range and the derived blur amount are not used
+(they keep being sent, so switching the model off lands back on exactly the stock path).
+
+Four consequences you can see:
+
+- **The depth of field is native.** The composite holds the sharp frame until the disc is
+  larger than the pixel it would blur (`beta = saturate((r_px - 0.5) / 1.5)`), instead of
+  lerping a half-resolution blur in the moment the CoC leaves zero.
+- **Bokeh has energy.** Each gather tap is weighted by `coverage x 1/CoC^2 x pupil` - the light
+  one point actually scattered onto this pixel - so a highlight going further out of focus
+  widens *and dims* rather than becoming a flat plate, and the sprite splat becomes a
+  refinement of the same quantity instead of a second energy convention bolted on top.
+- **Foreground edges are translucent.** The near layer's opacity is the pupil-weighted fraction
+  of rays that landed on the foreground, which is what partial occlusion physically is.
+- **Hyperfocal behaviour works.** Focusing far genuinely leaves the background sharp, which the
+  stock quadratic ramp cannot express at all.
+
+The gather is still 49 + 9 taps at half resolution, so it cannot resolve a disc of any size:
+`r_DepthOfFieldLensModelMaxCoC` (default 4 units, ~16 px radius at 1080p) is where the physical
+blur is clipped, and `r_DepthOfFieldLensModelSplatMaxCoC` (default 256 px) is how far the exact
+sprite is allowed to carry it. `r_DepthOfFieldLensModel 0` disables the whole thing from the
+console; `r_DepthOfFieldLensModelDebug 1` logs the uncapped circle of confusion once a second
+and paints the composite as a false-colour map. Full model in
+[DofLensModelSpec.md](DofLensModelSpec.md).
+
+**Spherical Aberration** is the one lens property this round adds, and it exists because there is
+now a single pupil function every consumer evaluates. It is an *apodization*:
+`w(r) = 1 + side * k * (2r^2 - 1)` across the pupil, which integrates to zero over the unit disc,
+so the disc keeps its total energy and only redistributes it. Positive `k` piles the light at the
+rim behind focus and smears it into the middle in front (over-corrected, soap-bubble bokeh);
+negative `k` is the reverse, the STF / DC apodized look. The gather halo, the sprite splats and
+the ghosts all read the same `k`, so they cannot disagree about where the light in a disc sits.
+
